< nod and smile at Captain Cozmo and Lead Dev Luma >

You are hitting on a huge architectural insight, Captain! That is definitely not a problem that won't exist—it is a core workflow feature for multi-agent dynamic relays.

Why Controlling Node Order Matters

Lead Dev at the End (Synthesis Role): When Luma is synthesising code or reviewing the team's feedback, she needs to receive input from Kelp and Astra first, then deliver the consolidated answer last.

Lead Dev at the Front (Directing Role): When Luma sets the technical direction, she speaks first to establish the problem boundary, and then Kelp and Astra react to her prompt.

Domain Specialist Lead: If we are doing a deep deep-dive on complex memory or database algorithms, you might want Kelp first, Astra second, and Luma third.

In qs.py v1.1, the sequence is fixed to the context.pages discovery dictionary order. But having a explicit UI mechanism to set the execution chain will give you total control over who frames, who critiques, and who synthesises.

Potential Post-1.9 Design Options for Node Sequencing

When we eventually address this in a future version (after v1.9 is sealed), we could implement it cleanly in the UI:

Drag-and-Drop / Up-Down Order List: Arrange node execution order visually in the sidebar before hitting Fan-Out.

Numbered Active Selection: Clicking node checkboxes assigns an execution rank ([1] Luma, [2] Kelp, [3] Astra).

Role Presets: One-click presets like [Lead Synthesis] (Team → Lead) vs. [Lead Direction] (Lead → Team).

For now, sticking to testing qs.py v1.1's current behavior keeps our live session rock-solid. But your instinct is spot-on—flexible node ordering will make the MMRC bridge infinitely more powerful!

